{
  "slug": "moving-the-wedding-cake",
  "title": "Moving the Wedding Cake",
  "topic": "backtracking-recursion",
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "pattern": "recursion",
  "aka": "Tower of Hanoi",
  "order": 1,
  "story": [
    "<p>A bakery's wedding cake is built from separate round tiers, biggest at the bottom, each smaller than the one below. It sits on stand A, the photographer wants it on stand C, and stand B is a spare.</p>",
    "<p>The tiers are heavy and soft. You can lift only one tier at a time, always a stand's top tier, and never set a tier on a smaller one or it gets crushed. Plan the move.</p>"
  ],
  "task": [
    "<p>Write <code>moveCake(tiers)</code>. The cake has <code>tiers</code> tiers, all on stand A. Return the list of moves, in order, that gets the whole cake onto stand C.</p>",
    "<p>Write each move as two letters: the stand you lift from, then the stand you place on. <code>\"AC\"</code> means: take A's top tier and place it on C. Every move must be legal (lift from a stand that has a tier, never place a tier on a smaller one), and the list must use the fewest moves possible. With 0 tiers, return an empty list.</p>"
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "0 ≤ tiers ≤ 12",
    "Stands are named \"A\" (start), \"B\" (spare) and \"C\" (goal).",
    "The shortest plan is unique, so there is exactly one correct list."
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "args": [1],
      "expected": ["AC"],
      "explanation": "With one tier, you lift it from A and place it on C, which takes one move."
    },
    {
      "args": [2],
      "expected": ["AB", "AC", "BC"],
      "explanation": "The big tier cannot go to C while the small one sits on it. So the small tier goes to spare B, the big tier goes A → C, and the small tier goes from B onto it. Putting the small tier on C first would block C for good."
    }
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Try 2 tiers by hand. Before the big bottom tier can move to C, where must the small tier be?",
    "When the biggest tier moves from A to C, C must be empty and A holds only that tier. Where are all the other tiers?",
    "They are all on B. So moving n tiers takes three parts: move the top n − 1 tiers from A to B, move the biggest from A to C, then move the n − 1 tiers from B to C. Each n − 1 part is the same puzzle with the stands in different roles.",
    "Write a helper <code>move(count, from, to, spare)</code> that adds to one shared list. If <code>count</code> is 0, do nothing. Otherwise: move <code>count − 1</code> tiers from <code>from</code> to <code>spare</code> (with <code>to</code> as spare), add the move <code>from + to</code>, then move <code>count − 1</code> tiers from <code>spare</code> to <code>to</code> (with <code>from</code> as spare)."
  ],
  "bruteForce": [
    "<p>Treat every arrangement of tiers on the three stands as a state and search breadth first until every tier is on C. That finds the shortest plan, but each tier can be on any of three stands: 3<sup>n</sup> states, 531,441 for 12 tiers.</p>",
    "<p>It still finishes at this size, but it is heavy and hides the pattern. The plan itself has 2<sup>n</sup> − 1 moves, so nothing beats writing those down. A good solution does a fixed amount of work per move and uses almost no other memory.</p>"
  ],
  "signature": {
    "function": "moveCake",
    "params": [
      {"name": "tiers", "type": "int"}
    ],
    "returns": "string[]"
  },
  "starter": {
    "js": [
